          Why You Must Reduce SVG File Size for Web Performance and Technical SEO
        </h3>
        <div class="mt-4 space-y-3">
          <p>
            Page speed is a primary Google search ranking signal. When you <strong>reduce svg file size</strong>, your website achieves tangible improvements across Google Core Web Vitals:
          </p>
          <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-5">
            <li>
              <strong>Largest Contentful Paint (LCP):</strong> Prominent vector graphics used in hero headers or logos influence LCP scores. Compressing heavy SVGs ensures fast first-paint times and higher search rankings.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Cumulative Layout Shift (CLS):</strong> Properly cleaned SVGs with explicit <code class="font-mono text-xs">viewBox</code> attributes prevent sudden visual reflows while graphics load.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>DOM Tree Efficiency:</strong> When inlining SVGs into React, Next.js, or Astro components, each shape adds a node to the DOM. A bloated graphic can add hundreds of unnecessary DOM elements. Using an <strong>svg minifier online</strong> keeps your DOM compact and prevents rendering stutter.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Bandwidth Savings on Mobile Networks:</strong> Streamlined vector assets load significantly faster on cellular connections, decreasing bounce rates among mobile visitors.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </article>

